PN,31701
SC,0001
CT,2,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F317A1786027701
OL,3,CDiscarded SpaceX rocket set to
OL,4,Cunintentionally smash into the moon
OL,6,FA drifting SpaceX rocket is set to
OL,7,Fcrash into the moon on Wednesday,
OL,8,Ftracking experts say.
OL,10,FThe rocket is on a collision course
OL,11,Fwith the moon after it launched a pair
OL,12,Fof lunar landers more than a year ago.
OL,14,FIts upper stage will unintentionally
OL,15,Fslam into Earth's only natural
OL,16,Fsatellite, carving out a crater and
OL,17,Fsending up a plume of dust and rubble.
OL,19,FThe upper stage is the final part of
OL,20,Fthe rocket that ignites, propelling it
OL,21,Finto space.
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G1/10
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,23,TppppppppppppppppppGsource: itv.com/news
OL,24,ANext Page BNat News CNat TV FClean Feed
FL,318,300,200,140,000,100
PN,31702
SC,0002
CT,2,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F317A1786027701
OL,3,CDiscarded SpaceX rocket set to
OL,4,Cunintentionally smash into the moon
OL,6,FFor scientists and skygazers eager to
OL,7,Fobserve the phenomenon, the crash will
OL,8,Foccur at 7.35am BST on Wednesday.
OL,10,FEastern areas in the US and Canada,
OL,11,Fand much of South America, are
OL,12,Fexpected to have the best views.
OL,14,FGround and space-based telescopes will
OL,15,Fbe aimed at the moon with the hopes of
OL,16,Fcapturing a glimpse, but there will
OL,17,Fnot be a way for the public to watch
OL,18,Fit live.
OL,20,FIt is, however, hoped that images of
OL,21,Fthe event will be captured.
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G2/10
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,23,TppppppppppppppppppGsource: itv.com/news
OL,24,ANext Page BNat News CNat TV FClean Feed
FL,318,300,200,140,000,100
PN,31703
SC,0003
CT,2,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F317A1786027701
OL,3,CDiscarded SpaceX rocket set to
OL,4,Cunintentionally smash into the moon
OL,6,FThe spacecraft is predicted to hit
OL,7,Fnear the Einstein Crater at an impact
OL,8,Fof 5,400 mph (8,700 kph), seven times
OL,9,Fthe speed of sound, and packing the
OL,10,Fequivalent energy of three tonnes of
OL,11,FTNT, according to Space tracking
OL,12,Fexpert Bill Gray.
OL,14,FAlthough scientists are not overly
OL,15,Fconcerned with the space junk's
OL,16,Fmovements, it highlights the growing
OL,17,Fthreat as more and more items cram
OL,18,Finto orbit.
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G3/10
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
OL,23,TppppppppppppppppppGsource: itv.com/news
OL,24,ANext Page BNat News CNat TV FClean Feed
FL,318,300,200,140,000,100
PN,31704
SC,0004
CT,2,C
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 1   F317A1786027701
OL,3,CDiscarded SpaceX rocket set to
OL,4,Cunintentionally smash into the moon
OL,6,F"Things are getting crowded up there,"
OL,7,Fsaid Gray, who plans to view the
OL,8,Faftermath from New Brunswick, Canada.
OL,10,FSpaceX never meant to hit the moon,
OL,11,Fbut experts suggest it could have been
OL,12,Favoided if that part of the rocket had
OL,13,Fbeen nudged into orbit around the sun.
OL,15,FThis will be the second dead rocket
OL,16,Fknown to crash into the moon
OL,17,Faccidentally. A Chinese rocket segment
OL,18,Fdug out a pair of craters on the lunar
OL,19,Ffar side in 2022.
OL,26,@nD@AOUnD@fO]
OL,1,W~k4~sj5j5~s1G                    G4/10
OL,2,W/*%+,"-/.!,.!T,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,,
